      <h2>How to Pick Your First One</h2>
      <p>The trap is buying the prettiest box. My Everdell tree was a $69.99 decoration for two months. Pretty sells, but pretty does not teach.</p>

      <p>The overkill is buying whatever a YouTube ranking calls the best game ever made. Best overall and best for your table are different questions, and the second one needs your actual friends in the room.</p>

      <p>The smart choice is a sequence. Kingdomino first, to find out if your group likes building. 5-Minute Dungeon or Castle Panic second, to find out if they like co-op. Wyrmspan third. Everdell after that, when the table can handle an 80 minute game without reaching for a phone.</p>

      <p>If your table is almost always 2 players, Kingdomino and 5-Minute Dungeon both work at that count, and Castle Panic holds up best of the three, since one or two people can run the whole defense without waiting on a partner.</p>

      <p>Here is why that matters. The first two games cost $49.73 together. That is less than the single box I bought first and never opened.</p>

      <h2>The Honest Truth</h2>
      <p>Here is the honest truth: Kingdomino goes thin. After about 20 plays you have seen most of the tile combinations, and the 2 player variant, where you run two kingdoms at once, feels bolted on. It is a $20 game and it behaves like one.</p>

      <p>I still tell every beginner to buy it, because the job of a first game is to get played, not to last a decade. When it goes stale, Wyrmspan is the natural next step.</p>

      <h2>The Shelf Test</h2>
      <p>The Shelf Test asks one question: would this game survive 10 plays on your shelf? Kingdomino, yes, spread across a year. 5-Minute Dungeon, yes, in one night. Castle Panic, yes if there are kids in the house. Wyrmspan, yes, and it will be the one you keep. Everdell, only if 2 people will commit to an 80 minute game on a weeknight. Spirit Island, not yet, and that is the honest reason to wait.</p>

      <p class="cta">If you are starting from zero, buy <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Kingdomino+board+game&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Kingdomino</a> and <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=5-Minute+Dungeon+board+game&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">5-Minute Dungeon</a>. That is $49.73 for a quiet night and a loud one. Kingdomino sits at $19.99 and bounces between $17 and $25 depending on the week, so anything under $20 is a good buy. Wyrmspan is the one you save up for, and it rarely drops below $55.</p>

      <p>If you want more games in the same lane, browse our <a href="/games/family">family games</a>, <a href="/games/cooperative">cooperative games</a>, and <a href="/games/strategy">strategy games</a> sections. If you are brand new to the hobby in general, the <a href="/blog/best-board-games-for-beginners">beginner list</a> starts one step earlier, and the <a href="/blog/how-to-play-wingspan">how to play Wingspan guide</a> will make Wyrmspan feel familiar before you open the box.</p>
